# ARMED AT BOOT, INDEPENDENT OF THE AGENT. This is the path that survives the
# agent being SIGKILLed, wedged, or never starting at all.
#
# 9300s = 1200 (max jitter) + 300 (Tor allowance) + 7200 + 600 (slack).
#
# 7200 IS THE LARGEST JOB THAT DOES NOT EXTEND THIS TIMER -- `watch`. It is
# NOT the largest job: `withdraw` is 58200s, and this number does not cover it
# and must not be raised to.
#
# The comment used to say "7200 (the largest job's budget) ... sized for the
# LARGEST job", which was true when every job only minted and watched. It is
# now wrong in both directions a reader could take it: someone could believe
# this backstop covers a withdrawal (it does not -- it would fire 9300s into a
# 58200s spend), or "correct" it to 60300 and leave the vault powered on for
# seventeen hours after an agent died during a two-minute status probe.
#
# WHY IT DOES NOT NEED TO COVER withdraw. gs_wake_agent.extend_deadman arms a
# TRANSIENT longer timer for spending jobs, verifies it is really active, and
# only then calls disarm_deadman() to stop THIS one -- so during a withdrawal
# this timer is not running. If the extension cannot be armed the agent
# refuses the job outright rather than starting a spend under a short
# backstop. Arm-verify-disarm, in that order, so there is no instant with no
# backstop at all.
#
# The stated cost of the number that remains: a boot whose agent DIED on a
# short job sits up to ~2h35m instead of ~20 min. The agent's own per-job
# budget bounds the normal case; generous-but-bounded is the right shape for a
# backstop that only runs when the thing that should have bounded it is dead.
[Unit]
Description=GhostSpiral wake deadman (power off even if the agent dies)

[Timer]
OnActiveSec=9300
AccuracySec=30s
Unit=gs-wake-poweroff.service

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
